"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.7. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3), pathological stage pT2, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. The absence of removed lymph nodes, noted as pNx, widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. Biopsy results showed a progression from Gleason 0+0 to Gleason 3+4, with perineural invasion noted in the second biopsy.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of extraprostatic extension and no seminal vesicle invasion. The positive surgical margins represent a risk factor. The lack of documented postoperative PSA or follow-up significantly limits the certainty of the trajectory. Missing nodal sampling further increases this uncertainty. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trend is crucial for refining this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 56.82 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 36.93–76.70 months; its coverage has not been validated."
